      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"regions\":{\"r2\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"idea, however, is included in \u0938\u094d\u0935\u0938\u094d\u0925 the reading \u0915\u0932\u0947\u0935\u0930\u0917\u0943\u0939\u0947 may be\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"preferred. The human body is frequently referred to as a\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"\u2018house\u2019, for therein dwells the soul. \u092f\u093e\u0935\u091a\u094d &c.\u2014and as long as\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"the senses retain their faculties unimpaired. The last line very\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"pithily expresses an idea which brings home to the most\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"careless reader the necessity for striving to save his soul.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"The Sanskrit readings in lines 1 and 3 are blurred.\"},\"r3\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Sl. 77. \u201cHuman life being only as long as a few twinklings\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"of the eye, how are we, the poet asks, to secure the four \u092a\u0941\u0930\u0941\u0937\u093e\u0930\u094d\u0925s\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"viz. \u0927\u0930\u094d\u092e, \u0905\u0930\u094d\u0925, \u0915\u093e\u092e, and \u092e\u094b\u0915\u094d\u0937 ?\u201d This seems to be what R\u00e1marshi\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"thinks of the verse. The reference to \u0905\u0930\u094d\u0925 in the text is of the\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"faintest character. \u0936\u0924\u0926\u0940\u092a-\u0935\u0938\u094d with \u0905\u0927\u093f governs the acc; \u0928\u093f\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"however, is not mentioned among the prepositions given in the\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":7,\"text\":\"s\u00fatra. Mark the alliteration in this sl.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"The Sanskrit grammatical quotation in line 5 is unclear.\"},\"r4\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Sl. 78. \u0905\u092e\u0940\u2014so well known. \u0924\u0941\u0930\u0917\u091a\u092a\u0932\u091a\u093f\u0924\u094d\u0924\u093e\u0903\u2014neither very\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"common nor a good simile; a horse is known for fleetness and\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"not for fickleness. It simply means here \u2018restless like a spirited\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"horse\u2019. Cf. B\u00e0na\u2019s description of Lakshmi\u2014\u0907\u092f\u0902 \u0939\u093f...\u0907\u0928\u094d\u0926\u0941\u0938\u0915\u0932\u093e\u0926\u0947-\",\"end\":\"hyphen\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"\u0915\u093e\u092e\u0932\u0935\u093f\u0932\u093e\u0938\u0948\u0903\u092d\u0935\u0924\u092d\u094d\u0930\u092e\u0924\u093e\u0902......\u0917\u0943\u0939\u0940\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e &c. \u092c\u0939\u0941\u092e\u0928\u0938\u0903\u2014longing for\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"(eminence). In line 4 the word \u0905\u0928\u094d\u092f\u0924\u094d\u0930 seems to be redundant,\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":7,\"text\":\"as we have already \u0905\u0928\u094d\u092f\u0924\u094d.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"The Sanskrit quotation in lines 4\u20135 is heavily blurred; several words are uncertain.\"},\"r5\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Sl. 79. \u0935\u0938\u0941\u0928\u093f \u0916\u0923\u094d\u0921\u093f\u0924\u0947\u2014R\u00e1marshi explains this as \u2018wealth\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"being partitioned out\u2019; in connection with the last two lines,\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"however, this primary meaning will not suit; and we must have\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"the secondary sense \u2018wealth being destroyed\u2019 here. \u0935\u094d\u092f\u0930\u094d\u0925\u092e\u094d\u2014\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"(\u0935\u093f+\u0905\u0930\u094d\u0925\u092e\u094d) without getting any money (in charity, for which\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"he comes). \u091c\u0939\u094d\u0928\u0941\u0915\u0928\u094d\u092f\u093e\u2014The Ganges is so called from the king\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":7,\"text\":\"Jahnu, \u2018The river Ganges, when brought down from heaven\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":8,\"text\":\"by the austerities of Bhag\u00edratha (See N\u2019i. S\u2019a. sl. 10.), was\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":9,\"text\":\"forced to flow over earth to follow him to the lower regions.\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":10,\"text\":\"In its course it inundated the sacrificial ground of king Jahnu\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":11,\"text\":\"who being angry drank up its waters. But the gods and sages,\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":12,\"text\":\"and particularly Bhag\u00edratha, appeased his anger, and he con-\",\"end\":\"hyphen\"},{\"n\":13,\"text\":\"sented to discharge those waters from his ears. The river is\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":14,\"text\":\"therefore regarded as his daughter.\u2019\u2019 Apte\u2019s Sk. Dictionary.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"The Sanskrit words in lines 1 and 4\u20135 are blurred.\"},\"r1\":{\"type\":\"page_number\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"( 72 )\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":false,\"note\":null}},\"units\":[{\"id\":\"u1\",\"type\":\"notes\",\"number\":null,\"label\":null,\"open_start\":true,\"open_end\":true,\"children\":[\"u2\",\"u3\",\"u4\",\"u5\"]},{\"id\":\"u2\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"76\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":true,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r2\"]},{\"id\":\"u3\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"77\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r3\"]},{\"id\":\"u4\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"78\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r4\"]},{\"id\":\"u5\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"79\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":true,\"children\":[\"r5\"]}],\"flows\":[{\"flow\":\"main\",\"units\":[\"u1\"]}],\"relations\":[]}